WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.570
<v Instructor>So if we look at a typical architecture</v>

2
00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:07.290
or network topology for your VPC, your customer gateway,

3
00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:09.900
your VPN connections, direct connect and so on,

4
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:11.790
it becomes very, very complicated

5
00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:14.310
because you allow some things to talk to one another,

6
00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:17.070
and anytime you need another thing to talk to another thing,

7
00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:19.320
then you have to create maybe a new VPN connection,

8
00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:20.910
maybe a new VPC Peering connection,

9
00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:23.550
and it can become very, very complicated.

10
00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:26.940
And so the idea is that as we grow our architectures,

11
00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:30.030
the number of accounts we have, we need to have a better way

12
00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:33.390
of handling all the network topology in our accounts.

13
00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:37.140
And we saw the VPC transits, that was a possible solution,

14
00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.600
but here we have a transit gateway

15
00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.530
which is going to be a more modern solution brought by AWS.

16
00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:46.050
So the reason we introduce a transit gateway

17
00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.810
is that we want to have transitive peering between,

18
00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:52.710
I would say thousands of VPC, even on premise data centers

19
00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:55.710
into a hub and spoke star connection.

20
00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:56.730
So why a star?

21
00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:59.220
Well, you can look at the diagRAM, it looks like a star.

22
00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:00.330
And so as you can see,

23
00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:04.230
my treasure gateway can connect many VPCs together.

24
00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:06.660
They can also connect to direct connect gateway

25
00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.990
if we needed to, and also connect to our on premise

26
00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:12.780
through a VPN connection and a customer gateway.

27
00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:16.470
So similar to the idea of the transit VPC we had before,

28
00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:19.680
now we have a transit gateway and all the traffic will flow

29
00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.290
through the transit gateway and we'll have control over

30
00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:25.200
how that traffic is going to be rerouted

31
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.170
to different endpoints in our star network.

32
00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:31.440
So the transit gateway is a regional resource,

33
00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.330
although you can peer together

34
00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:36.120
many transit gateways across region.

35
00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:37.980
And to share a transit gateway

36
00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:39.540
into many different accounts,

37
00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:42.330
you use resource access manager or RAM,

38
00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:44.160
and that is one of the primary use case

39
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.980
for using RAM alongside transit gateway.

40
00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:49.050
You can peer transit gateways across regions

41
00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:51.870
as where as just said, so it has cross region support.

42
00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:54.420
And in the route tables, you are able to limit

43
00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:57.180
which VPC can talk to which other VPC.

44
00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:58.770
So you define these route tables directly

45
00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:00.540
at the transit gateway level.

46
00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:03.390
It works with direct connect gateway VPN connections.

47
00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:07.500
And one thing it supports is called IP multicast.

48
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.570
So trendy gateway, to my knowledge,

49
00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:13.110
is the only thing that supports IP multicast

50
00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:15.300
in your AWS network.

51
00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:18.240
No other services will support IP multicast.

52
00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.760
And so if your architecture and your application

53
00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:22.530
is relying on IP multicast,

54
00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:25.410
then you have to use a transit gateway.

55
00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:27.510
So what does it give us, a transit gateway?

56
00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:29.580
Well, instances in the VPC

57
00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.920
can access any other NAT gateway,

58
00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:33.690
network load balancer or private link

59
00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:37.170
or EFS in other VPCs attached to the transit gateway.

60
00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:39.960
So here we do have edge to edge routing,

61
00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:42.860
we have transitivity and this is the whole purpose

62
00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:44.460
of the transit gateway.

63
00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:46.080
So let's go through an architecture

64
00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.120
in which we have the transit gateway

65
00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:49.920
be a central NAT gateway,

66
00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.710
and you really have to understand what's going on.

67
00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:55.980
So I would suggest you look at this link and open the blog,

68
00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:57.720
but I will walk you through this architecture

69
00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:58.890
as best as I can.

70
00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:01.260
But take your time, try to understand how that works

71
00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:04.320
because if you do, then you're good to go for VPCs.

72
00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:07.110
So the idea is that we have an Egress VPC

73
00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:10.890
on the left hand side, and within it, we have a NAT gateway.

74
00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.977
And so we want these NAT gateways into two AZs,

75
00:03:13.977 --> 00:03:18.420
so AZ one and AZ two, to take all the internet traffic

76
00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:21.030
from my other VPCs in my architecture.

77
00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:22.920
This is something I've tried to represent to you

78
00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:26.070
before in another slide, but I showed you it wasn't possible

79
00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:30.900
because of the lack of edge to edge routing for VPC peering.

80
00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.900
But here, so we have two NAT gateways and two AZ,

81
00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:35.520
and they're highly available.

82
00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:37.620
And then we have two ENIs,

83
00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:39.840
and these ENIs are going to allow us

84
00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:42.570
to connect our transit gateway.

85
00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:44.400
And so our transit gateway in here

86
00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:46.890
is going to be connected to two VPC,

87
00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:50.490
so the app1-VPC and the app2-VPC.

88
00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:53.580
And through the editing of the route tables,

89
00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:58.580
we are going to allow any traffic coming from the app2-VPC

90
00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:02.310
to go all the way through my Egress VPC,

91
00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:04.440
and get to the internet gateway here

92
00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:05.910
and talk to the internet.

93
00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:07.140
And why would we do this?

94
00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:09.600
Well, because we have more control here over

95
00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:12.180
how the internet traffic is going to be distributed.

96
00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:14.220
We also have the centralization of resources,

97
00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:16.590
so we don't to create one NAT gateway

98
00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:18.660
in one internet gateway per VPC,

99
00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:20.730
we can keep all the other VPCs private

100
00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:22.950
and make sure they have to access the internet

101
00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:27.150
through the transit gateway, through the Egress VPC.

102
00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:30.120
And so for this to work, you need to edit some route tables.

103
00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:33.030
So here, it says 000/0

104
00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:35.280
should go to the transit gateway internet

105
00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:36.900
and then the transit gateway internet is saying,

106
00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:40.350
okay, anything that comes from 000/0

107
00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:42.750
should go to these ENIs,

108
00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:45.270
and then it goes on to NAT gateway and so on.

109
00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:48.270
And you look at all the route tables and that works.

110
00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:49.950
But something that is happening

111
00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:54.030
in that diagram is that the app VPCs themselves,

112
00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:56.040
they cannot talk to each other.

113
00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:57.960
And the reason for that is that

114
00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:01.770
the transit gateway route table does not allow currently

115
00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:03.750
these VPCs to talk to one another,

116
00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:06.150
but that would be something that's very possible.

117
00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:08.850
So meaning that if you have a transit gateway,

118
00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:10.410
it doesn't necessarily mean that

119
00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:12.660
everything is going to be peered with another.

120
00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:14.700
You have to define the route tables

121
00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:17.760
to allow things to be talking to one another.

122
00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:20.040
So you can also share your transit gateway

123
00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:23.340
using the resource access manager service, RAM.

124
00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:25.170
And so you can share within other accounts

125
00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:28.020
or within your AWS organization.

126
00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:29.760
So let's say for example, we have an account A

127
00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:32.460
with a transit gateway that's being set up correctly,

128
00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:34.320
and then account B, well, first we can

129
00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:36.030
share our transit gateway,

130
00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:38.220
account B will then accept our request,

131
00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:41.640
and then the account B can just use the transit gateway

132
00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:44.370
for example, to create more attachments

133
00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:47.430
of VPC to the transit gateway, but VPC that belong

134
00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:50.160
in account B, therefore simplifying

135
00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:51.633
your network architecture.

136
00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:56.070
Also using the transit gateway, you can connect it to

137
00:05:56.070 --> 00:05:57.930
many different accounts, many different VPCs.

138
00:05:57.930 --> 00:06:00.270
So using the resource access manager,

139
00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:04.560
we can go into some sort of network topology where

140
00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:06.270
different VPCs from different accounts

141
00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:08.670
are connected to the same transit gateway.

142
00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:11.130
But of course, you do not want the prod

143
00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:13.260
and the staging account to communicate together

144
00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:14.790
or the dev and the pre-product account

145
00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:16.350
to communicate together.

146
00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:17.790
So in that case, what you can do

147
00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:20.910
is that you can create, for example, a prod route table

148
00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:23.280
which gives some CIDRs as well as the next hop,

149
00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:26.700
which is a special transit gateway attachment.

150
00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:29.820
And if you attach it to these one,

151
00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:33.150
the A and B VPC in the prod and pre-prod accounts,

152
00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:37.110
we only ensure that VPC A can communicate with with VPC B

153
00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:38.340
and vice versa.

154
00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:40.260
But C and D are isolated from it

155
00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:43.440
because they do not appear in this route table.

156
00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:45.810
Likewise, we could create a staging route table

157
00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:48.330
with the correct transit gateway attachments

158
00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:50.160
to ensure that the staging and the dev accounts

159
00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:51.240
can communicate with each other,

160
00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:53.610
but not with the prod accounts.

161
00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:54.840
So by default, of course,

162
00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:55.860
everything will connect to each other,

163
00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:58.890
but if you're to create your own route tables

164
00:06:58.890 --> 00:07:00.960
and attach them to the transit gateway,

165
00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:04.770
you can specify which VPCs or which attachments

166
00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:06.483
can route to each other.

167
00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:09.720
If you have a direct connect gateway set up,

168
00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:11.580
so you have corporate data center,

169
00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:15.090
a direct connect location, and a direct connect gateway,

170
00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:16.890
you can connect to multiple regions.

171
00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:20.070
So if you create a transit gateway in us-east-1

172
00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:23.190
and a transit gateway in us-west-2 in two different regions,

173
00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:26.670
it is totally possible for you to connect the gateway

174
00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:28.260
to the first transit gateway

175
00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:31.500
as well as the second transit gateway.

176
00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:35.190
Therefore, you have a direct connection into

177
00:07:35.190 --> 00:07:38.610
multiple regions through using a transit gateway.

178
00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:42.090
And of course, if the us-east-1 VPCs

179
00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:44.970
must talk with the us-west-2 VPCs,

180
00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:49.380
you can create an inter-region transit gateway peering

181
00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:51.210
to peer the true transit gateway together

182
00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:53.940
and then allow the network connectivity

183
00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:56.673
to go directly and stay within AWS.

184
00:07:57.570 --> 00:08:00.210
So which brings me to the second subject, which is

185
00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:03.180
around inter and intra region peering.

186
00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:05.190
So say we have a region right here,

187
00:08:05.190 --> 00:08:08.220
we create a transit gateway, we can do attachments

188
00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:12.600
to site to site VPN to VPCs and so on.

189
00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:14.940
But we can also, for example, create a second

190
00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:18.390
transit gateway or use a third party transit gateway

191
00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:20.430
and connect connected to its own, of course,

192
00:08:20.430 --> 00:08:22.110
type of topology.

193
00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:25.110
And you can establish an intra-region peering mesh.

194
00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:27.990
So you have your own VPC transit gateway

195
00:08:27.990 --> 00:08:32.990
or TGW one connected to your second transit gateway.

196
00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:35.460
You can do the same thing in another region.

197
00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:36.293
So it's very possible for you

198
00:08:36.293 --> 00:08:37.680
to have the same kind of architecture.

199
00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:41.610
But if you wanted to connect all these regions together,

200
00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:43.980
you could create a third transit gateway

201
00:08:43.980 --> 00:08:46.080
which is a hub transit gateway,

202
00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:48.150
and then you connect it inter-region

203
00:08:48.150 --> 00:08:50.640
using an inter-region peering mesh.

204
00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:53.100
And then these transit gateways, the hub ones,

205
00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:55.350
can be connected to the ones within the region

206
00:08:55.350 --> 00:08:57.600
using in-region peering mesh.

207
00:08:57.600 --> 00:08:59.820
So you go into this kind of architecture

208
00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:02.490
and you can extend it into a third region

209
00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:05.310
to have, again, a mesh of inter-region peering mesh

210
00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:07.560
between your hub transit gateways,

211
00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:11.220
and within each region you have intra-region peering.

212
00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:12.810
So you're going to be billed hourly

213
00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:14.820
for each peering attachment,

214
00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:17.340
but there are no data processing charges.

215
00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:18.173
Okay?

216
00:09:18.173 --> 00:09:20.490
But if the data, of course, grows cross region

217
00:09:20.490 --> 00:09:21.870
through the attachments,

218
00:09:21.870 --> 00:09:23.880
then you will have standard charges.

219
00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:26.400
So if the data goes from region A to region B,

220
00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:28.260
of course, you're going to pay some money.

221
00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:31.140
For this to work, you must of course, do all these peerings

222
00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:34.800
by specifying the transit gateway ID.

223
00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:35.633
Okay.

224
00:09:35.633 --> 00:09:37.920
So that's it for transit gateway, I hope you liked it.

225
00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:39.870
And I will see you in the next lecture.

